Key components of proposed approach:
- Framework: factorial ablation on the official InfLLM codebase (training-free), base model fixed, benchmark suite of long-context tasks across lengths.
- Ablation axis A (dynamic context memory lookup):
  A1. No lookup / full-block attention: attention over all memory blocks (removes selection). 
  A2. Static lookup: top-k by fixed order (first k blocks) or uniform random, matched k — isolates "dynamic/content-based" from "having a budget".
  A3. Local-only (recent window only): removes memory blocks entirely — establishes upper-bound importance of memory.
  A4. Sensitivity: sweep k (top-k size) and temperature/sampling.
  Additionally, budget-matched comparisons to separate lookup quality from compute budget.
- Ablation axis B (unit representation):
  B1. Learned attention-pooling block representation (InfLLM default).
  B2. Mean-pooling of token K/V.
  B3. Last-token-only.
  B4. No compression oracle (raw per-token attention over entire context within compute limits) — upper bound.
  Also separate the TWO roles: representation for retrieval scoring vs. representation for within-block attention. Cross ablations: learned rep for retrieval + mean-pool for attention, etc.
