## 0. Objective
Estimate the causal, marginal contribution of two InfLLM components — (a) the **Dynamic Context Memory lookup** (per-step, content-based retrieval of memory blocks) and (b) the **unit (block-level) representation** (the compressed vector each memory block is reduced to) — to InfLLM's performance across long-context task families and context lengths. The design is a factorial, budget-matched ablation on the official InfLLM method, evaluated with paired-delta statistics, length/position curves, and a pre-registered interpretation rubric that maps observed delta magnitudes to "criticality" conclusions. This is a **design-only** deliverable: no experiment is run and no results are claimed.

## 1. Factors, Conditions, and Factorial Matrix

**Factor A — Memory lookup protocol (retrieval behavior), unit representation held at B0.**
- **A0** Full InfLLM: dynamic, query-based block retrieval at each decoding step (reference) — per-step scoring of all block unit vectors against the current key, selection of k blocks, plus the always-attended recent block.
- **A1** No-lookup / flat all-block attention: selection removed; every memory block contributes uniformly (same unit representation, same recent block). Captures the total effect of *having any selective budget*.
- **A2** Content-blind static retrieval: same budget k as A0 but selection is content-blind — two sub-variants: **A2-pos** (first-k by position) and **A2-rand** (uniform-random k, fixed per instance/seed). Isolates the *content-dynamic* component from the mere existence of a budget.
- **A3** Local-only (recent window only, no memory blocks): **lower-bound control** establishing the total value of the memory itself vs. a purely local baseline.
- **A4** Sensitivity sweeps: vary k (e.g., 8/32/64/128/all); vary selection greediness vs. score-temperature (0 → high); includes A2-rand as the fully non-dynamic extreme. Used to confirm conclusions are robust to the retrieval protocol's unknown exact settings (see Assumptions).

**Factor B — Unit representation (block-level compression), retrieval protocol held at A0.**
- **B0** Learned attention-pooling BlockRepresentation (InfLLM default).
- **B1** Mean-pooling of token K/V.
- **B2** Last-token-only.
- **B3** Raw/uncompressed per-token attention over the full context (only within base-LM training length) — **upper-bound / ceiling** control for what any compressed unit representation could in principle preserve.

**Two-role decomposition of B.** The unit vector serves two distinct functions: **(B-r)** the *retrieval key* (block scoring for lookup) and **(B-a)** the *attention content* used within retrieved blocks. We therefore run crossed conditions on the default vs. strongest alternative, e.g. **(B1r,B0a)** = mean-pool keys for retrieval + learned units for attention, and **(B0r,B1a)** = the reverse. This attributes the unit-vector's effect to its lookup role versus its attention-content role.

**Full factorial matrix.** A × B = 5×4 = 20 cells, plus the two crossed role conditions (22 total). Because the raw oracle (B3) is only valid within the base-LM training length, B3 is run only at the ≤ training-length regime and reported as a ceiling reference, not part of the main attribution.

**Pruning protocol.** Run the full 22-condition grid on a **probe set** (1–2 representative tasks per family at one fixed length, e.g. 16k, × 3 seeds). Select survivors = A0 plus (i) the best-performing variant per axis and (ii) all conditions needed for the interpretation rubric's contrasts (A2, A3, B0, B1, B2, crossed roles) — ~8–10 conditions. The survivors proceed to the full suite × length sweep.

## 2. Benchmark Suite and Metrics
Selected to span task types that exercise memory lookup and unit fidelity differently:

| Family | Benchmarks (assumed versions) | Metric |
|---|---|---|
| Retrieval / localization | ∞Bench Passkey; RULER needle-in-haystack | Accuracy / hit-rate; needle-depth curves |
| Multi-hop QA | LongBench HotpotQA, 2WikiMultihopQA | F1 |
| Single-/multi-doc QA | L-Eval single-long/multi-doc | F1 |
| Summarization | LongBench gov_report, multifieldqa | Rouge-L |
| Few-shot / knowledge recall | LongBench TREC, triviaqa | Accuracy / F1 |
| Code completion | LongBench repo, lcc | EditSim / pass@1 |

**Length sweep:** 2k / 4k / 8k / 16k / 32k / 64k / 128k (upper bound set by the base model and projected GPU VRAM, see Feasibility). This sweep operationalizes "across long-context tasks": per-family aggregations plus score-vs-length curves per condition. Needle-depth (position) curves are produced for retrieval tasks to show where lookup matters (mid-context vs. near endpoints).

## 3. Controls
- Same base LM (candidate: Llama-2-7B; see Assumptions) with **all weights frozen** in every condition.
- Identical decoding (greedy, temperature 0), fixed max_new_tokens, identical prompt templates and evaluation harness.
- **Fixed test instances** (same seeds for any sampling; same random-block orderings for A2-rand) so all comparisons are paired.
- Identical block size (assumed 128), recent-window size (assumed 1024), and a **matched retrieval budget k** across compared A-variants.
- A1 and A3 inherently use different compute/KV than A0; these are additionally compared under a **budget-matched** projection (same number of attended tokens) so that "selection quality" is not confounded with "compute spent."
- **Params policy (default):** all compared variants keep the pretrained BlockRepresentation (and any added module) **frozen**, preserving the training-free comparison. Retraining a representation variant (e.g., re-train the pooling query for B1/B2) is permitted **only as a clearly labeled secondary condition** (costing a short GPU run), never the default, and separately reported so it never pollutes the main attribution.

## 4. Analysis Plan
- **Primary statistic:** paired per-instance deltas Δ = score(A0,B0) − score(condition), per family and overall, with **bootstrap 95% CIs over instances** (≈10k resamples) and variance across seeds.
- **Marginal lookup contribution Δ_A:** A0 minus best same-B non-dynamic variant at matched budget (contrast A0 vs A2 primarily); A0 vs A3 quantifies total memory benefit; A0 vs A1 (budget-matched) separates selection quality from budget effect.
- **Marginal unit-representation contribution Δ_B:** B0 minus best alternative B at matched lookup (B0 vs B1, B0 vs B2); B0 vs B3 (in-length) quantifies headroom.
- **Role attribution:** crossed conditions (B1r/B0a vs B0r/B1a) isolate retrieval-key vs. attention-content roles of the unit vector.
- **Interaction:** report whether Δ_A depends on B and Δ_B depends on A (two-factor contrast on paired deltas); an interaction plot plus a stated conclusion on dependence, per the rubric below.
- **Curves:** score-vs-context-length curves per surviving condition; needle-depth curves for retrieval tasks; k-sensitivity and selection-temperature curves from A4.

## 5. Feasibility / Cost (projected requirements, not executed results)
- **Execution is out of scope in this workspace:** local runtime is CPU-only (Python 3.14, no torch/CUDA, no network, no data, no InfLLM source; only task.json present, no track.md). The projected figures below describe what running the design on a GPU cluster (e.g., 8×A100-80GB, official thunlp/InfLLM repo, installed via pip with an allowed HuggingFace mirror) would require.
- **Probe phase:** 22 conditions × ~6 tasks × 1 length × 3 seeds ≈ ~400 inference runs of a 7B model — order of tens of GPU-hours total (projected, dominated by longer contexts).
- **Full phase:** ~8–10 survivor conditions × ~12 task instances per family × 7 lengths × 3–5 seeds ≈ several hundred GPU-hours on a single A100-class GPU (projected); 128k contexts are the most expensive tier and can be reduced to 64k if VRAM is limited.
- **Secondary retraining condition (labeled, non-default):** short-context block-representation training for B1/B2 variants ≈ a few GPU-hours on a single A100 for 7B (projected).
- **Statistical protocol:** 3–5 seeds; paired bootstrap over instances; results reported as mean ± 95% CI with a fixed significance threshold (p < 0.05 on the bootstrap interval excluding zero).

## 6. Assumptions and Limitations
- **Method-detail assumptions (not locally verifiable, flagged as assumptions with a sensitivity note):** the official InfLLM retrieval uses query-to-block-representation scoring with hard or temperature-sampled selection (k assumed ≈ 16–64); block size assumed 128; recent-window assumed 1024; base model assumed Llama-2-7B; benchmarks assumed LongBench v1, ∞Bench v1, RULER with default RULER/config parameters. **Sensitivity note:** the design includes A4 (k and temperature sweeps) and multiple families so that any stated conclusion is required to hold across the swept range of these unknown settings, not just at one assumed value.
- **Interpretation assumptions:** "training-free" refers to a frozen base LM; added-module training counts only in the labeled secondary condition. "Criticality" is defined by the pre-registered rubric below, not by a single p-value.
- **Limitations (acknowledged):** this is a design deliverable and produces no numbers; conclusions are conditional on realized deltas per the rubric. B3 (raw attention) cannot be extended beyond the base-LM training length, so the unit-representation ceiling is only measured in-length and extrapolated with caution. CPU-only execution in this workspace prevents running the experiment here.

## 7. Interpretation Rubric (pre-registered mapping from observed deltas to conclusions)
Define the **attainable range** R = A3(lower bound) → B3/ceiling (upper bound) per metric. Thresholds are fixed before data are seen. With bootstrap 95% CIs that exclude zero:
- **Critical (dominant):** |Δ| ≥ 0.5×R, per family or overall.
- **Moderate:** 0.2×R ≤ |Δ| < 0.5×R.
- **Minor / negligible:** |Δ| < 0.2×R or CI includes zero.

**Lookup conclusions (from A contrasts, at matched budget):**
- If A0 vs A2 ∈ Critical → dynamic content-based selection is the primary driver of lookup value; the memory benefit is not merely "having a budget."
- If A0 vs A2 is Minor but A0 vs A1 is Moderate/Critical → the budget (attention over a bounded block subset) matters; its content-adaptive choice does not.
- If A0 vs A3 is Minor → the memory itself contributes little on that family; lookup criticality is then per-family context-dependent.

**Unit-representation conclusions (from B contrasts):**
- B0 vs B1/B2 ∈ Critical → the learned unit representation is decisive; mean/last-token pooling is not interchangeable.
- B0 vs B3 ∈ Critical → large headroom remains from compression; the unit choice caps achievable fidelity.
- Crossed-role result: if gains concentrate in (B0r,B_x a) vs (B_x r,B0a) → the unit vector matters through its retrieval-key role vs. its attention-content role, which is reported explicitly.

**Interaction conclusions:**
- If Δ_A is Critical at B0 but Minor at the best alternative B (or vice versa) → lookup and unit representation are interacting (not independently critical); report the interaction cell deltas and plot, and state the combined conclusion: the two components' value is entangled and must be co-tuned.
- If Δ_A and Δ_B are each Critical and roughly additive → the components are independently critical; both are required for InfLLM's performance.

**Final deliverable format:** a table of per-condition mean ± CI per family and overall, the Δ_A / Δ_B margins and interaction contrast, length and needle-depth curves, a per-family criticality verdict, and a one-paragraph answer to the research question, each cell explicitly flagged as "projected design expectation" (no claim of executed results).", "evidence": "Repository inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (433 bytes), confirming a design-only task and referencing a track.md that is NOT present on disk (find / -name track.md returned nothing). No source code, checkpoints, datasets, or GPU were found: system Python 3.14.4 with no torch/CUDA packages, no GPU environment variables, network disabled, CPU-only bubblewrap with 224 CPUs. All InfLLM method details in this design (BlockRepresentation units, Dynamic Context Memory query-based retrieval, recent block, training-free w.r.t. base LM) come from the documented public InfLLM method (thunlp/InfLLM, EMNLP 2024) and are treated as assumptions, not locally verified facts. The workspace provides no constraints beyond the task statement, so the design is self-contained.", "assumptions": ["The reference 'full InfLLM' (A0/B0) is the official thunlp/InfLLM implementation: BlockRepresentation unit vectors, Dynamic Context Memory query-based block retrieval, and a persistent recent block, with the base LM frozen.", "Training-free means frozen base LM; the BlockRepresentation module may only be retrained in a clearly labeled, non-default secondary condition.", "Official retrieval details (hard vs. temperature-sampled top-k, block size ~128, recent-window ~1024, k ~16-64 for Llama-2-7B) are assumed, with the A4 sweep guaranteeing conclusions hold across the plausible setting range.", "Benchmark versions assumed (LongBench v1, ∞Bench v1, RULER defaults); base model assumed Llama-2-7B (with 13B as a robustness extension).", "Design-only: no experiment is run; feasibility/cost figures are projected requirements, not executed results, and execution is out of scope in the CPU-only workspace.", "Paired per-instance comparisons with bootstrap CIs are valid because test instances, seeds, decoding, block size, recent window, and retrieval budget are fixed across conditions."], "uncertainties": ["Exact official retrieval protocol (hard vs. sampled top-k; whether all blocks are scored every step; how the recent block merges with retrieval) is not locally verifiable; hedged by the A4 sensitivity sweep.", "Whether the block-rep module can be retrained per representation variant within the chosen compute budget; the default policy keeps it frozen and retraining is a labeled secondary condition.", "Target base model/config (7B vs 13B) and context-length ceiling depend on projected GPU VRAM; lengths 64k-128k are the costliest tier and may be truncated.", "Exact benchmark versions and metric-aggregation protocols (LongBench v1 vs v2, ∞Bench v1/v2, RULER config) are assumed and should be pinned in the official repos before execution.", "Whether budget-matched (attended-token/FLOPs) comparisons are fully implementable alongside top-k-matched ones, and the exact seed/instance budget for statistical power under constrained compute."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-5e4fcf460ad34b8f9bebb7b81a0d8008", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-5e4fcf460ad34b8f9bebb7b81a0d8008", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "# Final Experiment Design: Criticality of Dynamic Context Memory Lookup and Unit Representations in InfLLM\n\n## 0. Objective\nEstimate the causal, marginal contribution of two InfLLM components \u2014 (a) the **Dynamic Context Memory lookup** (per-step, content-based retrieval of memory blocks) and (b) the **unit (block-level) representation** (the compressed vector each memory block is reduced to) \u2014 to InfLLM's performance across long-context task families and context lengths. The design is a factorial, budget-matched ablation on the official InfLLM method, evaluated with paired-delta statistics, length/position curves, and a pre-registered interpretation rubric that maps observed delta magnitudes to \"criticality\" conclusions. This is a **design-only** deliverable: no experiment is run and no results are claimed.\n\n## 1. Factors, Conditions, and Factorial Matrix\n\n**Factor A \u2014 Memory lookup protocol (retrieval behavior), unit representation held at B0.**\n- **A0** Full InfLLM: dynamic, query-based block retrieval at each decoding step (reference) \u2014 per-step scoring of all block unit vectors against the current key, selection of k blocks, plus the always-attended recent block.\n- **A1** No-lookup / flat all-block attention: selection removed; every memory block contributes uniformly (same unit representation, same recent block). Captures the total effect of *having any selective budget*.\n- **A2** Content-blind static retrieval: same budget k as A0 but selection is content-blind \u2014 two sub-variants: **A2-pos** (first-k by position) and **A2-rand** (uniform-random k, fixed per instance/seed). Isolates the *content-dynamic* component from the mere existence of a budget.\n- **A3** Local-only (recent window only, no memory blocks): **lower-bound control** establishing the total value of the memory itself vs. a purely local baseline.\n- **A4** Sensitivity sweeps: vary k (e.g., 8/32/64/128/all); vary selection greediness vs. score-temperature (0 \u2192 high); includes A2-rand as the fully non-dynamic extreme. Used to confirm conclusions are robust to the retrieval protocol's unknown exact settings (see Assumptions).\n\n**Factor B \u2014 Unit representation (block-level compression), retrieval protocol held at A0.**\n- **B0** Learned attention-pooling BlockRepresentation (InfLLM default).\n- **B1** Mean-pooling of token K/V.\n- **B2** Last-token-only.\n- **B3** Raw/uncompressed per-token attention over the full context (only within base-LM training length) \u2014 **upper-bound / ceiling** control for what any compressed unit representation could in principle preserve.\n\n**Two-role decomposition of B.** The unit vector serves two distinct functions: **(B-r)** the *retrieval key* (block scoring for lookup) and **(B-a)** the *attention content* used within retrieved blocks. We therefore run crossed conditions on the default vs. strongest alternative, e.g. **(B1r,B0a)** = mean-pool keys for retrieval + learned units for attention, and **(B0r,B1a)** = the reverse. This attributes the unit-vector's effect to its lookup role versus its attention-content role.\n\n**Full factorial matrix.** A \u00d7 B = 5\u00d74 = 20 cells, plus the two crossed role conditions (22 total). Because the raw oracle (B3) is only valid within the base-LM training length, B3 is run only at the \u2264 training-length regime and reported as a ceiling reference, not part of the main attribution.\n\n**Pruning protocol.** Run the full 22-condition grid on a **probe set** (1\u20132 representative tasks per family at one fixed length, e.g. 16k, \u00d7 3 seeds). Select survivors = A0 plus (i) the best-performing variant per axis and (ii) all conditions needed for the interpretation rubric's contrasts (A2, A3, B0, B1, B2, crossed roles) \u2014 ~8\u201310 conditions. The survivors proceed to the full suite \u00d7 length sweep.\n\n## 2. Benchmark Suite and Metrics\nSelected to span task types that exercise memory lookup and unit fidelity differently:\n\n| Family | Benchmarks (assumed versions) | Metric |\n|---|---|---|\n| Retrieval / localization | \u221eBench Passkey; RULER needle-in-haystack | Accuracy / hit-rate; needle-depth curves |\n| Multi-hop QA | LongBench HotpotQA, 2WikiMultihopQA | F1 |\n| Single-/multi-doc QA | L-Eval single-long/multi-doc | F1 |\n| Summarization | LongBench gov_report, multifieldqa | Rouge-L |\n| Few-shot / knowledge recall | LongBench TREC, triviaqa | Accuracy / F1 |\n| Code completion | LongBench repo, lcc | EditSim / pass@1 |\n\n**Length sweep:** 2k / 4k / 8k / 16k / 32k / 64k / 128k (upper bound set by the base model and projected GPU VRAM, see Feasibility). This sweep operationalizes \"across long-context tasks\": per-family aggregations plus score-vs-length curves per condition. Needle-depth (position) curves are produced for retrieval tasks to show where lookup matters (mid-context vs. near endpoints).\n\n## 3. Controls\n- Same base LM (candidate: Llama-2-7B; see Assumptions) with **all weights frozen** in every condition.\n- Identical decoding (greedy, temperature 0), fixed max_new_tokens, identical prompt templates and evaluation harness.\n- **Fixed test instances** (same seeds for any sampling; same random-block orderings for A2-rand) so all comparisons are paired.\n- Identical block size (assumed 128), recent-window size (assumed 1024), and a **matched retrieval budget k** across compared A-variants.\n- A1 and A3 inherently use different compute/KV than A0; these are additionally compared under a **budget-matched** projection (same number of attended tokens) so that \"selection quality\" is not confounded with \"compute spent.\"\n- **Params policy (default):** all compared variants keep the pretrained BlockRepresentation (and any added module) **frozen**, preserving the training-free comparison. Retraining a representation variant (e.g., re-train the pooling query for B1/B2) is permitted **only as a clearly labeled secondary condition** (costing a short GPU run), never the default, and separately reported so it never pollutes the main attribution.\n\n## 4. Analysis Plan\n- **Primary statistic:** paired per-instance deltas \u0394 = score(A0,B0) \u2212 score(condition), per family and overall, with **bootstrap 95% CIs over instances** (\u224810k resamples) and variance across seeds.\n- **Marginal lookup contribution \u0394_A:** A0 minus best same-B non-dynamic variant at matched budget (contrast A0 vs A2 primarily); A0 vs A3 quantifies total memory benefit; A0 vs A1 (budget-matched) separates selection quality from budget effect.\n- **Marginal unit-representation contribution \u0394_B:** B0 minus best alternative B at matched lookup (B0 vs B1, B0 vs B2); B0 vs B3 (in-length) quantifies headroom.\n- **Role attribution:** crossed conditions (B1r/B0a vs B0r/B1a) isolate retrieval-key vs. attention-content roles of the unit vector.\n- **Interaction:** report whether \u0394_A depends on B and \u0394_B depends on A (two-factor contrast on paired deltas); an interaction plot plus a stated conclusion on dependence, per the rubric below.\n- **Curves:** score-vs-context-length curves per surviving condition; needle-depth curves for retrieval tasks; k-sensitivity and selection-temperature curves from A4.\n\n## 5. Feasibility / Cost (projected requirements, not executed results)\n- **Execution is out of scope in this workspace:** local runtime is CPU-only (Python 3.14, no torch/CUDA, no network, no data, no InfLLM source; only task.json present, no track.md). The projected figures below describe what running the design on a GPU cluster (e.g., 8\u00d7A100-80GB, official thunlp/InfLLM repo, installed via pip with an allowed HuggingFace mirror) would require.\n- **Probe phase:** 22 conditions \u00d7 ~6 tasks \u00d7 1 length \u00d7 3 seeds \u2248 ~400 inference runs of a 7B model \u2014 order of tens of GPU-hours total (projected, dominated by longer contexts).\n- **Full phase:** ~8\u201310 survivor conditions \u00d7 ~12 task instances per family \u00d7 7 lengths \u00d7 3\u20135 seeds \u2248 several hundred GPU-hours on a single A100-class GPU (projected); 128k contexts are the most expensive tier and can be reduced to 64k if VRAM is limited.\n- **Secondary retraining condition (labeled, non-default):** short-context block-representation training for B1/B2 variants \u2248 a few GPU-hours on a single A100 for 7B (projected).\n- **Statistical protocol:** 3\u20135 seeds; paired bootstrap over instances; results reported as mean \u00b1 95% CI with a fixed significance threshold (p < 0.05 on the bootstrap interval excluding zero).\n\n## 6. Assumptions and Limitations\n- **Method-detail assumptions (not locally verifiable, flagged as assumptions with a sensitivity note):** the official InfLLM retrieval uses query-to-block-representation scoring with hard or temperature-sampled selection (k assumed \u2248 16\u201364); block size assumed 128; recent-window assumed 1024; base model assumed Llama-2-7B; benchmarks assumed LongBench v1, \u221eBench v1, RULER with default RULER/config parameters. **Sensitivity note:** the design includes A4 (k and temperature sweeps) and multiple families so that any stated conclusion is required to hold across the swept range of these unknown settings, not just at one assumed value.\n- **Interpretation assumptions:** \"training-free\" refers to a frozen base LM; added-module training counts only in the labeled secondary condition. \"Criticality\" is defined by the pre-registered rubric below, not by a single p-value.\n- **Limitations (acknowledged):** this is a design deliverable and produces no numbers; conclusions are conditional on realized deltas per the rubric. B3 (raw attention) cannot be extended beyond the base-LM training length, so the unit-representation ceiling is only measured in-length and extrapolated with caution. CPU-only execution in this workspace prevents running the experiment here.\n\n## 7. Interpretation Rubric (pre-registered mapping from observed deltas to conclusions)\nDefine the **attainable range** R = A3(lower bound) \u2192 B3/ceiling (upper bound) per metric. Thresholds are fixed before data are seen. With bootstrap 95% CIs that exclude zero:\n- **Critical (dominant):** |\u0394| \u2265 0.5\u00d7R, per family or overall.\n- **Moderate:** 0.2\u00d7R \u2264 |\u0394| < 0.5\u00d7R.\n- **Minor / negligible:** |\u0394| < 0.2\u00d7R or CI includes zero.\n\n**Lookup conclusions (from A contrasts, at matched budget):**\n- If A0 vs A2 \u2208 Critical \u2192 dynamic content-based selection is the primary driver of lookup value; the memory benefit is not merely \"having a budget.\"\n- If A0 vs A2 is Minor but A0 vs A1 is Moderate/Critical \u2192 the budget (attention over a bounded block subset) matters; its content-adaptive choice does not.\n- If A0 vs A3 is Minor \u2192 the memory itself contributes little on that family; lookup criticality is then per-family context-dependent.\n\n**Unit-representation conclusions (from B contrasts):**\n- B0 vs B1/B2 \u2208 Critical \u2192 the learned unit representation is decisive; mean/last-token pooling is not interchangeable.\n- B0 vs B3 \u2208 Critical \u2192 large headroom remains from compression; the unit choice caps achievable fidelity.\n- Crossed-role result: if gains concentrate in (B0r,B_x a) vs (B_x r,B0a) \u2192 the unit vector matters through its retrieval-key role vs. its attention-content role, which is reported explicitly.\n\n**Interaction conclusions:**\n- If \u0394_A is Critical at B0 but Minor at the best alternative B (or vice versa) \u2192 lookup and unit representation are interacting (not independently critical); report the interaction cell deltas and plot, and state the combined conclusion: the two components' value is entangled and must be co-tuned.\n- If \u0394_A and \u0394_B are each Critical and roughly additive \u2192 the components are independently critical; both are required for InfLLM's performance.\n\n**Final deliverable format:** a table of per-condition mean \u00b1 CI per family and overall, the \u0394_A / \u0394_B margins and interaction contrast, length and needle-depth curves, a per-family criticality verdict, and a one-paragraph answer to the research question, each cell explicitly flagged as \"projected design expectation\" (no claim of executed results).", "assumptions": ["The reference 'full InfLLM' (A0/B0) is the official thunlp/InfLLM implementation: BlockRepresentation unit vectors, Dynamic Context Memory query-based block retrieval, and a persistent recent block, with the base LM frozen.", "Training-free means frozen base LM; the BlockRepresentation module may only be retrained in a clearly labeled, non-default secondary condition.", "Official retrieval details (hard vs. temperature-sampled top-k, block size ~128, recent-window ~1024, k ~16-64 for Llama-2-7B) are assumed, with the A4 sweep guaranteeing conclusions hold across the plausible setting range.", "Benchmark versions assumed (LongBench v1, \u221eBench v1, RULER defaults); base model assumed Llama-2-7B (with 13B as a robustness extension).", "Design-only: no experiment is run; feasibility/cost figures are projected requirements, not executed results, and execution is out of scope in the CPU-only workspace.", "Paired per-instance comparisons with bootstrap CIs are valid because test instances, seeds, decoding, block size, recent window, and retrieval budget are fixed across conditions."], "evidence": "Repository inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (433 bytes), confirming a design-only task and referencing a track.md that is NOT present on disk (find / -name track.md returned nothing). No source code, checkpoints, datasets, or GPU were found: system Python 3.14.4 with no torch/CUDA packages, no GPU environment variables, network disabled, CPU-only bubblewrap with 224 CPUs. All InfLLM method details in this design (BlockRepresentation units, Dynamic Context Memory query-based retrieval, recent block, training-free w.r.t. base LM) come from the documented public InfLLM method (thunlp/InfLLM, EMNLP 2024) and are treated as assumptions, not locally verified facts. The workspace provides no constraints beyond the task statement, so the design is self-contained.", "stage": "result", "summary": "Hand off complete design-only experiment plan for InfLLM ablation", "uncertainties": ["Exact official retrieval protocol (hard vs. sampled top-k; whether all blocks are scored every step; how the recent block merges with retrieval) is not